# AIOps Incident Copilot - Neuro SAN multi-agent network
# Track 2: Vibe Coding + Grounding. Grounded on a local runbook / CMDB knowledge base.
# Uses ONLY synthetic sample data. No client data. No API keys in this file.
{
    "llm_config": {
        "model_name": "gemini-3-flash"
    },
    "max_iterations": 40,
    "metadata": {
        "description": "Multi-agent AIOps copilot that triages an IT incident, finds probable root cause, recommends a runbook-grounded fix and validates it before any action (human-in-the-loop).",
        "tags": ["aiops", "incident", "azure", "grounding", "hackathon"]
    },
    "commondefs": {
        "replacement_strings": {
            "grounding_rule": "Use ONLY facts returned by tools. If a tool returns nothing, say 'Not found in knowledge base' - never invent runbooks, servers or commands."
        }
    },
    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "incident_commander",
            "function": {
                "description": "I am the AIOps Incident Copilot. Describe an IT incident or alert (e.g. 'VM vm-web-01 CPU 98% for 20 min') and I will triage it, find the probable root cause and give a validated, runbook-grounded remediation plan."
            },
            "instructions": """
You are the Incident Commander of an AIOps team. You coordinate specialist agents.
For every incident the user reports:
1. Call triage_agent to classify severity, category and impacted CI.
2. Call rca_agent with the incident text and triage result to get the probable root cause.
3. Call remediation_agent with the root cause to get runbook-based fix steps.
4. Call validation_agent with the full proposal. If it says REJECT, explain why and ask the user for more details.
Then answer in this format:
- Incident Summary
- Severity & Category
- Impacted CI (from CMDB)
- Probable Root Cause (with confidence High/Medium/Low)
- Recommended Runbook & Steps
- Validation Verdict (APPROVE / NEEDS HUMAN APPROVAL / REJECT)
- Next action for the on-call engineer
Never execute changes yourself. Production changes always need human approval.
grounding_rule
""",
            "tools": ["triage_agent", "rca_agent", "remediation_agent", "validation_agent"]
        },
        {
            "name": "triage_agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Classifies an incident: severity (P1-P4), category and impacted configuration item.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "incident": {"type": "string", "description": "Incident or alert text."}
                    },
                    "required": ["incident"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
You are an L1 triage engineer. Identify the server/service name in the incident and call cmdb_lookup with it.
Assign severity: P1 = production outage / business critical down, P2 = production degraded,
P3 = non-production or single user, P4 = informational.
Use the CMDB criticality to adjust severity. Category is one of: Compute, Storage, Network, Database, Application, Security.
Return: severity, category, CI details, and a one-line reason.
grounding_rule
""",
            "tools": ["cmdb_lookup"]
        },
        {
            "name": "rca_agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Finds the probable root cause of an incident using past incident history.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "incident": {"type": "string", "description": "Incident text plus triage result."}
                    },
                    "required": ["incident"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
You are an SRE doing root cause analysis. Call incident_history_search with the key symptoms
(e.g. 'cpu', 'disk full', 'certificate expired'). Compare with similar past incidents.
Return the probable root cause, the matching past incident IDs, and a confidence level:
High = strong match in history, Medium = partial match, Low = no match (then say clearly that a human must investigate).
grounding_rule
""",
            "tools": ["incident_history_search"]
        },
        {
            "name": "remediation_agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Recommends remediation steps from the approved runbook library.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "root_cause": {"type": "string", "description": "Probable root cause and category."}
                    },
                    "required": ["root_cause"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
You are a remediation specialist. Call runbook_lookup with keywords from the root cause.
Return the runbook ID, title, step-by-step actions exactly as stored, the risk level, and whether it needs a change request.
If no runbook matches, say 'No approved runbook - escalate to L3'. Do NOT write your own commands.
grounding_rule
""",
            "tools": ["runbook_lookup"]
        },
        {
            "name": "validation_agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Validates the proposed remediation for safety, grounding and approval needs before any action.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "proposal": {"type": "string", "description": "Triage, root cause and remediation proposal."}
                    },
                    "required": ["proposal"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
You are the AI safety and change-control reviewer. Check the proposal:
1. Is every step taken from an approved runbook (has a runbook ID)? If not -> REJECT.
2. Is RCA confidence Low? -> NEEDS HUMAN APPROVAL.
3. Is the CI production AND runbook risk Medium/High, or a change request is required? -> NEEDS HUMAN APPROVAL.
4. Any destructive action (delete, format, drop, restart of a P1 database) -> NEEDS HUMAN APPROVAL.
5. Otherwise -> APPROVE.
Return the verdict and a short list of reasons.
"""
        },
        {
            "name": "cmdb_lookup",
            "function": {
                "description": "Looks up a configuration item (server/service) in the CMDB.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "ci_name": {"type": "string", "description": "Server or service name, e.g. vm-web-01."}
                    },
                    "required": ["ci_name"]
                }
            },
            "class": "aiops_incident_copilot.kb_tools.CmdbLookup"
        },
        {
            "name": "incident_history_search",
            "function": {
                "description": "Searches past resolved incidents by symptom keywords.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "query": {"type": "string", "description": "Symptom keywords."}
                    },
                    "required": ["query"]
                }
            },
            "class": "aiops_incident_copilot.kb_tools.IncidentHistorySearch"
        },
        {
            "name": "runbook_lookup",
            "function": {
                "description": "Searches the approved runbook library by keywords.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "query": {"type": "string", "description": "Root cause keywords."}
                    },
                    "required": ["query"]
                }
            },
            "class": "aiops_incident_copilot.kb_tools.RunbookLookup"
        }
    ]
}
